# Routes org/auth guard work to the canonical model.
#
# IMS tokens are ORG-BOUND, and this is the single fact that ad-hoc org handling
# keeps rediscovering. There is one approach here and no in-app org picker.
rule_id=org-context
rule_once=1

rule_match() {
    case "$1" in
        Edit|Write) ;;
        *) return 1 ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$3" ] || return 1
    case "$3" in
        */services/ensureOrgContext.ts) return 0 ;;
        */services/detectProjectOrgMismatch.ts) return 0 ;;
        */orgContextEnv.ts) return 0 ;;
        */services/authenticationService.ts) return 0 ;;
    esac
    return 1
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
You are editing Adobe org/auth handling. Invoke the `adobe-org-context` skill
(Skill tool, skill: "adobe-org-context") first — it is the canonical model, and
ad-hoc org comparisons are the thing it exists to prevent.

The load-bearing fact: IMS tokens are ORG-BOUND. A token issued for one org does
not work for another, which is why the flow is `ensureOrgContext` +
`detectProjectOrgMismatch` + per-operation `withOrgContext` targeting, with a
forced re-login ("Switch IMS Org") as the recovery. There is NO in-app org picker
and no place to compare org ids by hand.

Related trap worth knowing before you read a response: a named field in an API
response is a LEAD, not a finding. `ACCS-REST-API … enabled: false` was read as
"this org has no entitlement"; reading the same catalog in the other org is what
made the claim safe to write down.

This hook fires only once per session.
MSG
}
